E=[]
# --- part 1: Classical Greece & Rome
E+=[
X('alexander-the-great','Alexander the Great: conqueror, son of a god, and a tomb nobody can find',H,'confirmed',1,-336,['textual','archaeological','numismatic'],
 "Alexander III of Macedon (356–323 BCE) became king in 336 BCE and within about a decade defeated the Persian Achaemenid Empire, took Egypt (where the oracle of Ammon at Siwa reportedly greeted him as son of the god), and marched as far as the Punjab, where his army refused to go further after the battle of the Hydaspes (326 BCE). He died in Babylon in June 323 BCE aged 32. His empire split among his generals, but the Greek-speaking kingdoms that followed spread Hellenistic culture from Egypt to Afghanistan. His body was taken to Egypt and displayed in Alexandria for centuries; the location of the tomb has been lost since late antiquity.",
 [('How did he die?','Ancient sources disagree. Modern suggestions include malaria, typhoid fever, alcohol-related illness, poisoning (an ancient rumour blamed Antipater\'s sons) and, in a 2018 proposal by physician Katherine Hall, Guillain–Barré syndrome; none can be confirmed without remains.'),
  ('Legend: the Alexander Romance','From late antiquity onward the Alexander Romance turned him into a hero who descended under the sea in a glass bell, flew in a basket drawn by griffins and searched for the water of life. Versions spread in dozens of languages from Iceland to Ethiopia and Malaysia.'),
  ('Dhul-Qarnayn and Iskandar','Many traditional and modern commentators identify the Qur\'anic Dhul-Qarnayn ("the two-horned"), who builds a wall against Gog and Magog, with Alexander; others reject this identification. In Persian tradition (Shahnameh) he appears as Iskandar, sometimes as a half-Persian prince, while Zoroastrian texts remember him as "the accursed" destroyer.'),
  ('The lost tomb','Excavators have searched Alexandria and the Siwa oasis for the tomb; claims to have found it (e.g. at Siwa in 1995) have not been accepted. Some writers have speculated that his remains were moved or misidentified as those of another figure; this is unproven.')],
 ['wiki:Alexander_the_Great','wiki:Alexander_Romance','wiki:Death_of_Alexander_the_Great','wiki:Tomb_of_Alexander_the_Great'],img='Alexander_the_Great',end=-323,dateNote='reigned 336–323 BCE'),
X('eratosthenes-earth','Eratosthenes measures the Earth (c. 240 BCE)',H,'confirmed',2,-240,['textual'],
 "Eratosthenes of Cyrene, head of the Library of Alexandria, estimated the circumference of the Earth around 240 BCE. According to the later account of Cleomedes, he noted that at Syene (Aswan) the noon sun at the summer solstice stood almost directly overhead, while at Alexandria it cast a shadow corresponding to about one-fiftieth of a circle (7.2°). Taking the distance between the two cities as 5,000 stadia, he obtained 250,000 stadia (later given as 252,000). Depending on which length of stadion he used, his result was within roughly 2% to 16% of the modern value of about 40,000 km. He also produced a map of the inhabited world, a system of latitude and longitude lines and the 'sieve' method for finding prime numbers.",
 [('Exact accuracy is uncertain','Because the stadion varied (about 157 to 185 m), and Syene is not exactly on the Tropic of Cancer or due south of Alexandria, historians debate whether his good result reflects skill, luck or both.'),
  ('The "flat-Earth Middle Ages" myth','Educated Europeans throughout the Middle Ages knew the Earth was round. The popular idea that Columbus\'s contemporaries believed in a flat Earth was largely spread in the 19th century, notably by Washington Irving\'s 1828 biography of Columbus; the real dispute with Columbus was about the Earth\'s size, where Eratosthenes-type estimates were closer to correct.'),
  ('Modern flat-Earth belief','Small modern flat-Earth movements reject the measurement; repeated experiments, including school replications of Eratosthenes\' method, satellite imagery and navigation, all support a spherical Earth.')],
 ['wiki:Eratosthenes','wiki:Myth_of_the_flat_Earth'],img='Eratosthenes',dateNote='c. 240 BCE'),
X('thermopylae','Thermopylae (480 BCE): the 300 Spartans and the numbers game',H,'confirmed',2,-480,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In August or September 480 BCE a Greek force of several thousand men under the Spartan king Leonidas, including his 300 Spartans, held the narrow coastal pass of Thermopylae against the invading army of the Persian king Xerxes for three days. According to Herodotus, a local man named Ephialtes showed the Persians a mountain path that let them outflank the Greeks; Leonidas sent most allies away and died with the Spartans, Thespians and Thebans who remained. The Greek navy fought simultaneously at Artemisium, and the Persians were defeated at Salamis later that year and at Plataea in 479 BCE.",
 [('How big was Xerxes\' army?','Herodotus gives over 2.6 million fighting men (more than 5 million with support). Modern historians consider this impossible and usually estimate somewhere between about 70,000 and 300,000.'),
  ('Only 300?','The famous "300" counts only Spartan citizens; ancient sources indicate roughly 5,000–7,000 Greeks at the start, and helots and other allies also died there. The film 300 (2006) is based on a graphic novel and is not intended as history.'),
  ('Heroic sacrifice or tactical retreat?','Some historians read the last stand as a deliberate rearguard to cover the Greek withdrawal; others see it as rooted in Spartan law and religion (an oracle reportedly said Sparta or its king must fall).')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Thermopylae','wiki:Leonidas_I'],img='Battle_of_Thermopylae',dateNote='August or September 480 BCE'),
X('mask-of-agamemnon','The "Mask of Agamemnon": the wrong king, and a debated authenticity',H,'disputed',3,-1550,['archaeological'],
 "In 1876 Heinrich Schliemann excavated Grave Circle A at Mycenae and found gold funerary masks in the shaft graves. On finding one in Shaft Grave V, he is often reported (from later accounts) to have declared he had 'gazed on the face of Agamemnon'. The graves date to about 1600–1500 BCE, some 300 years before the time traditionally assigned to the Trojan War, so the mask cannot belong to the Agamemnon of Homer, if he existed. It is now displayed in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens.",
 [('Genuine Mycenaean mask','Most archaeologists accept the mask as an authentic Late Helladic object, stylistically different from the other masks but within Mycenaean variation.'),
  ('Partly or wholly faked?','In the 1970s William M. Calder III and David Traill argued that Schliemann, who had a record of embellishing his finds, may have had the mask made or altered (for example its upturned moustache). Specialists have mostly rejected this, but the mask has never been scientifically tested to settle the question.'),
  ('Homer\'s heroes as history','The find fed a long-running debate on whether Homer\'s epics preserve memories of real Bronze Age kings; Linear B tablets and Hittite texts (Ahhiyawa, Wilusa) show a real Mycenaean world, but no named Homeric king has been confirmed.')],
 ['wiki:Mask_of_Agamemnon','wiki:Grave_Circle_A,_Mycenae','wiki:Heinrich_Schliemann'],img='Mask_of_Agamemnon',dateNote='mask c. 1550–1500 BCE; found 1876',
 changed='1876: hailed as the face of Agamemnon. 20th c.: redated to c. 1550–1500 BCE, centuries before any Trojan War. 1972 onward: authenticity questioned by Calder and Traill; still debated but mostly accepted as genuine.'),
X('sibylline-books','The Sibylline Books: Rome\'s secret oracles, burned twice',H,'disputed',2,-520,['textual'],
 "The Sibylline Books were a collection of Greek oracular verses kept in the temple of Jupiter on the Capitoline Hill in Rome and consulted only on the Senate's order by a priestly college (later the quindecimviri sacris faciundis) in times of crisis, such as plagues, prodigies and military disasters. Their advice typically prescribed new rites or the import of foreign cults, such as that of Cybele in 204 BCE. The original collection burned with the temple in 83 BCE; a new one was gathered from across the Mediterranean, moved by Augustus to the temple of Apollo on the Palatine, and, according to the poet Rutilius Namatianus, was burned by the general Stilicho in the early 5th century CE.",
 [('The legend of the purchase','Roman tradition says a mysterious old woman, the Sibyl of Cumae, offered nine books to king Tarquinius; when he refused her price she burned three, then three more, and he finally bought the last three for the original price. Historians regard this as a foundation legend.'),
  ('Political tool or genuine prophecy?','Ancient writers such as Cicero already suspected the books could be interpreted to suit those in power; believers saw them as divinely inspired. Their contents were kept secret and almost nothing of the original text survives.'),
  ('Not the Sibylline Oracles','A separate surviving collection called the Sibylline Oracles was written largely by Jewish and Christian authors from about the 2nd century BCE onward; it is sometimes confused with the lost Roman books.')],
 ['wiki:Sibylline_Books','wiki:Cumaean_Sibyl','wiki:Sibylline_Oracles'],img='File:Francesco Salviati - Scene from the story of Tarquinius and the Sibylline Books - NMH 163-1863 - Nationalmuseum.jpg',end=405,dateNote='traditionally acquired 6th c. BCE; burned 83 BCE; destroyed early 5th c. CE'),
X('caesars-comet','Caesar\'s Comet (44 BCE): a murdered dictator becomes a star',H,'confirmed',2,-44,['textual','numismatic','astronomical'],
 "In July 44 BCE, a few months after Julius Caesar was assassinated, a bright comet was seen in Rome for seven days during the games held in his honour by his adopted heir Octavian. Romans widely interpreted it as Caesar's soul ascending to join the gods. Octavian (later Augustus) promoted it as the sidus Iulium ('Julian star'), placed a star on statues of Caesar and minted coins showing it, supporting Caesar's official deification in 42 BCE. Chinese records also describe a comet in 44 BCE, which some astronomers consider the same object.",
 [('A real comet','Astronomers generally accept that a genuine, unusually bright (possibly sungrazing) comet appeared; it is only known from historical records and has no modern identification.'),
  ('Political propaganda','Historians emphasise how Octavian exploited the omen to legitimise his position; some have questioned how widely it was seen in Rome, as the Roman reports are mostly from writers favourable to him.'),
  ('Comets as omens worldwide','The episode fits a much wider pattern in which comets and new stars were read as signs of the death or rise of rulers, from Chinese court astrology to the 1066 comet on the Bayeux Tapestry.')],
 ['wiki:Caesar\'s_Comet','wiki:Assassination_of_Julius_Caesar'],img='File:Comet Caesar (Roman denarius).png',dateNote='July 44 BCE'),
X('teutoburg-forest','Teutoburg Forest (9 CE): three legions lost, and a battlefield found by a metal detectorist',H,'confirmed',2,9,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In September 9 CE an alliance of Germanic tribes led by Arminius, a Cheruscan noble who had served as an officer with the Roman army, ambushed and destroyed three Roman legions (XVII, XVIII and XIX) and auxiliaries under the governor Publius Quinctilius Varus in northern Germany. Augustus is said to have cried 'Quintilius Varus, give me back my legions!' The legion numbers were never reused, and Rome never permanently held Germania east of the Rhine. From 1987, after finds of Roman coins and sling-shot by British officer Tony Clunn, excavations at Kalkriese near Osnabrück uncovered a battlefield with Roman equipment, bones and a turf rampart used by the attackers.",
 [('Is Kalkriese the site?','Most archaeologists identify Kalkriese with the Varus battle; a minority argue it may instead record fighting during Germanicus\' campaigns in 15–16 CE, since the finds are hard to date to a single year.'),
  ('Turning point or overstatement?','It is often said to have kept Germany outside the Roman (and later Romance-language) world; some historians argue Roman expansion was already slowing for financial and strategic reasons.'),
  ('A national myth','In the 19th century "Hermann" (Arminius) became a German national hero; the Hermannsdenkmal monument was completed in 1875, and the battle was later used in nationalist propaganda.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_the_Teutoburg_Forest','wiki:Kalkriese','wiki:Arminius'],img='Battle_of_the_Teutoburg_Forest',dateNote='September 9 CE; Kalkriese excavations from 1987'),
X('ninth-legion','The vanishing Ninth Legion',H,'disputed',2,108,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Legio IX Hispana served in Britain from the invasion of 43 CE and built the fortress at York, where an inscription of 108 CE is the last firmly dated evidence for it in Britain. By the later 2nd century it no longer appears in lists of legions. For much of the 19th and 20th centuries scholars, following Theodor Mommsen, assumed it was destroyed in northern Britain around 117 CE, an idea popularised by Rosemary Sutcliff's novel The Eagle of the Ninth (1954). Later finds of stamped tiles and other material at Nijmegen in the Netherlands suggest that at least part of the legion was transferred to the Rhine.",
 [('Lost in Britain','Some historians still argue for a disaster in northern Britain in the 110s–120s, possibly connected with unrest before Hadrian\'s Wall was built.'),
  ('Lost in the East','Others propose the legion was destroyed later elsewhere, for example in the Bar Kokhba revolt in Judaea (132–136 CE) or at the start of the Parthian war (c. 161 CE); no direct evidence names it in either.'),
  ('Disbanded rather than destroyed','A further view is that the legion was disbanded or merged; there is no surviving record of its end, which is why the mystery persists.')],
 ['wiki:Legio_IX_Hispana','wiki:The_Eagle_of_the_Ninth'],img='Legio_IX_Hispana',dateNote='last dated inscription at York 108 CE; disappears by c. 165 CE',
 changed='19th c.: Mommsen proposes destruction in Britain c. 117 CE. 1954: Sutcliff\'s novel fixes the image of a legion lost in Scotland. Late 20th c.: Nijmegen finds suggest transfer to the Continent; fate still unknown.'),
X('liqian-roman-legion','Liqian: did lost Roman soldiers settle in China?',H,'hypothesis',2,-36,['textual','genetic','speculative'],
 "In 1955–57 the American sinologist Homer Dubs proposed that Roman soldiers captured by the Parthians after Crassus's defeat at Carrhae (53 BCE) later served as mercenaries for the Xiongnu leader Zhizhi and were captured by Han Chinese forces at the battle of Zhizhi (36 BCE), where Chinese sources describe a 'fish-scale formation' and a double palisade. He suggested they were then settled in a Han county called Liqian (Li-jien), a name similar to a Han term for the Roman or Hellenistic West. Villagers in Zhelaizhai, Gansu province, some with light hair or eyes, have since been linked to the story by media and local tourism.",
 [('Mainstream scepticism','Most historians regard Dubs\'s chain of identifications as unproven: the Chinese texts do not say the soldiers were Roman, the formation and palisade are not uniquely Roman, and no Roman artefacts have been found at Liqian.'),
  ('Genetic tests','A 2007 Y-chromosome study of men from the Liqian area found mostly East Asian paternal lineages and concluded the data did not support a Roman-soldier origin; varied appearance in north-west China is generally attributed to long-standing Silk Road and Central Asian ancestry.'),
  ('Still possible','Supporters argue that a small group of foreign captives could have been absorbed without leaving clear genetic or archaeological traces; the hypothesis therefore remains open but speculative.')],
 ['doi:10.1007/s10038-007-0155-0','wiki:Liqian','wiki:Battle_of_Zhizhi','wiki:Battle_of_Carrhae'],img='Battle_of_Carrhae',dateNote='Battle of Zhizhi 36 BCE; Dubs\'s hypothesis 1955–57'),
X('hypatia','Hypatia of Alexandria (killed 415 CE)',H,'confirmed',2,415,['textual'],
 "Hypatia was a Neoplatonist philosopher, astronomer and mathematician in Alexandria, daughter of the mathematician Theon. She taught pupils including Synesius of Cyrene (later a Christian bishop, whose letters to her survive) and edited or commented on mathematical works. In March 415 CE she was murdered by a Christian mob during a bitter political conflict between the Roman prefect Orestes, whom she advised, and the bishop Cyril of Alexandria. The main sources are the church historian Socrates Scholasticus, who condemned the killing, and later writers such as John of Nikiu, who justified it.",
 [('Martyr of reason','From the Enlightenment onwards writers such as Voltaire and Edward Gibbon, and later the film Agora (2009), presented her as a symbol of science destroyed by religious fanaticism, sometimes wrongly linking her death to the destruction of the Library of Alexandria.'),
  ('Victim of local politics','Many modern historians stress that she was killed as part of a power struggle in the city rather than for her philosophy or sex, and that pagan and Christian students studied with her.'),
  ('Cyril\'s responsibility','How far Bishop Cyril (later a saint) was responsible is disputed: some ancient and modern writers blame him directly, others see the mob as acting without his explicit order.')],
 ['wiki:Hypatia','wiki:Theon_of_Alexandria'],img='File:Hypatia (Charles William Mitchell).jpg',dateNote='born c. 350–370 CE; killed March 415 CE'),
]
# --- part 2: medieval Europe & the Islamic world
E+=[
X('codex-gigas','Codex Gigas, the "Devil\'s Bible"',H,'confirmed',2,1229,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Codex Gigas is the largest surviving medieval European manuscript, about 92 cm tall and weighing around 75 kg. It was produced in the early 13th century, probably at the Benedictine monastery of Podlažice in Bohemia, and contains the Vulgate Bible, Josephus's Jewish Antiquities, Isidore's Etymologies, medical texts, a chronicle of Bohemia, magical formulas and a calendar. One full page shows a large horned devil, opposite a page depicting the Heavenly City. Swedish troops took it from Prague in 1648 at the end of the Thirty Years' War; it is now in the National Library of Sweden in Stockholm.",
 [('Legend: a pact with the Devil','A later legend says a monk who had broken his vows was to be walled up alive, promised to write a book containing all human knowledge in a single night, and called on the Devil to finish it, adding the portrait in gratitude.'),
  ('One scribe, many years','Palaeographic studies indicate the whole book was written in a remarkably uniform hand by a single scribe, which modern researchers estimate would have taken roughly 20 to 30 years of work; a note in the manuscript names Herman the Recluse (Hermannus inclusus), traditionally taken to be the scribe.'),
  ('Why the devil?','Scholars generally read the devil opposite the Heavenly City as a didactic contrast between damnation and salvation, perhaps linked to the confession texts nearby, rather than evidence of diabolical authorship.')],
 ['wiki:Codex_Gigas'],img='File:Codex Gigas fol 290r Devil.jpg',dateNote='early 13th c. (c. 1204–1230); taken to Stockholm 1648'),
X('rok-runestone','The Rök runestone: heroic legend, or fear of a climate catastrophe?',H,'disputed',2,800,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Rök runestone in Östergötland, Sweden, was carved in the early 9th century and carries the longest known runic inscription, about 760 characters, on all its faces, partly written in cipher runes and different runic alphabets. It was raised by a man named Varin in memory of his dead son Vämod. The text poses a series of riddling questions and mentions a ruler of the Goths, usually identified with the Ostrogothic king Theodoric the Great, riding his horse.",
 [('Heroic legends','The traditional interpretation, developed from the 19th century, reads the text as a collection of allusions to heroic legends and battles, perhaps meant to show the dead son\'s connection to a great tradition.'),
  ('Fear of Fimbulwinter','A 2020 interdisciplinary study by Per Holmberg and colleagues argued that the riddles refer to the sun, darkness and winter, and that the stone expresses fear of a climate catastrophe like the cooling after the eruptions of 536–540 CE, linked to Norse ideas of Ragnarök. Critics consider this one possible reading among several.'),
  ('Cipher and magic','Some researchers see the cipher runes as playful scholarly display; others suggest ritual or magical intentions. The inscription remains one of the most debated texts of the Viking Age.')],
 ['doi:10.33063/diva-401040','wiki:Rök_runestone'],img='Rök_runestone',dateNote='early 9th c. CE'),
X('lambton-worm','The Lambton Worm: a dragon, a crusader and a nine-generation curse',M,'myth-legend',3,1420,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "The Lambton Worm is a legend from County Durham in north-east England. Young John Lambton skipped church to fish in the River Wear and caught a small eel-like 'worm', which he threw into a well before leaving for the Crusades. The creature grew huge, coiled around a hill (identified with Worm Hill near Fatfield), and devoured livestock and children. Returning years later, Lambton killed it wearing armour studded with spearheads, on the advice of a wise woman, but broke his vow to kill the first living thing he met afterwards, which was his father, and so his family was cursed for nine generations not to die in their beds. The story was recorded in the 19th century, including in Robert Surtees's History of Durham and a popular 1867 song.",
 [('Folk memory of real deaths?','Supporters of a historical core note that some Lambton family members did die violently or unusually; folklorists consider the curse a later explanation attached to these deaths.'),
  ('A dragon-slaying pattern','Folklorists place the tale among British "worm" legends (such as the Laidly Worm of Spindleston Heugh and the Sockburn Worm) and the wider dragon-slayer tradition, often tied to local landowning families and landmarks.'),
  ('Real animals or fossils?','Some writers have suggested the legend was inspired by large eels, escaped exotic animals or fossil finds; there is no evidence for these specific origins, which remain speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Lambton_Worm'],img='Lambton_Worm',dateNote='legend set in the 14th–15th c.; recorded in the 19th c.'),
X('golem-of-prague','The Golem of Prague',M,'myth-legend',2,1580,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "In Jewish folklore a golem is a human-like figure made of clay or earth and brought to life by sacred words or divine names; the idea draws on the Talmud (which tells of a rabbi who created a man) and on mystical speculation about the Sefer Yetzirah. The most famous version credits Rabbi Judah Loew ben Bezalel (the Maharal of Prague, died 1609) with creating a golem to defend Prague's Jewish community against blood-libel accusations; it was deactivated by removing the letter or name that animated it. The story linking the golem to Rabbi Loew appears in print only in the 19th century; earlier golem stories were attached to Rabbi Elijah of Chełm.",
 [('Legend versus the historical Maharal','Historians note that Rabbi Loew was a real and influential scholar, but none of his writings or contemporary sources mention a golem.'),
  ('The golem in the attic','A popular tradition holds that the golem\'s remains lie in the attic of Prague\'s Old New Synagogue; the attic has been visited (for example by journalist Egon Erwin Kisch in the 1920s) without finding any remains.'),
  ('Ancestor of robots and AI','The golem is often cited, together with Frankenstein and Karel Čapek\'s R.U.R. (1920, which coined "robot"), as a cultural ancestor of fears and hopes about artificial beings that escape their makers\' control.')],
 ['wiki:Golem','wiki:Judah_Loew_ben_Bezalel'],img='Golem',dateNote='attributed to the late 16th c.; Prague legend printed in the 19th c.'),
X('malleus-maleficarum','Malleus Maleficarum (1486) and the European witch hunts',H,'confirmed',2,1486,['textual'],
 "The Malleus Maleficarum ('Hammer of Witches') was written by the German Dominican inquisitor Heinrich Kramer (Institoris) and printed in 1486; the theologian Jacob Sprenger was named as co-author, though his role is disputed. It argued that witchcraft was real, heretical and mostly practised by women, and gave procedures for prosecuting witches. It reprinted Pope Innocent VIII's bull Summis desiderantes affectibus (1484). It went through many editions, although the Church never officially adopted it. Across Europe, witch trials between about 1450 and 1750 led to an estimated 40,000 to 60,000 executions, concentrated in the Holy Roman Empire.",
 [('How influential was it?','Some historians see the book as a major engine of the witch hunts; others argue it was one text among many and that most prosecutions were driven by local secular courts, community tensions, wars and climate stresses such as the Little Ice Age.'),
  ('"Nine million witches"','A figure of nine million victims, traceable to an 18th-century extrapolation and repeated by some 20th-century writers, is rejected by modern historians, whose archival studies support tens of thousands.'),
  ('Suppression of folk healers?','Some writers argue the trials targeted midwives, healers or surviving pagan practices; historians have found limited evidence that midwives were especially targeted, and the "surviving pagan witch-cult" theory of Margaret Murray (1921) has been largely rejected.')],
 ['wiki:Malleus_Maleficarum','wiki:Heinrich_Kramer','wiki:Witch_trials_in_the_early_modern_period'],img='Malleus_Maleficarum',dateNote='printed 1486'),
X('bayeux-comet-1066','Halley\'s Comet on the Bayeux Tapestry (1066)',H,'confirmed',2,1066,['textual','iconographic','astronomical'],
 "In April 1066 Halley's Comet was visible across Europe and Asia. The Bayeux Tapestry, an embroidered cloth about 70 m long probably made in England in the 1070s, shows people pointing at it with the caption 'ISTI MIRANT STELLA' ('these men wonder at the star'), next to the newly crowned King Harold II, months before his defeat and death at the Battle of Hastings on 14 October 1066. The chronicler William of Malmesbury later reported that the monk Eilmer of Malmesbury, who had once tried to fly with wings, greeted the comet as a sign of disaster for England.",
 [('Omen in hindsight','Historians note that the tapestry was made after the Norman victory and frames the comet as a portent of Harold\'s fall, consistent with its role as Norman-commissioned (probably by Bishop Odo) account of the conquest.'),
  ('A propaganda document?','Some scholars find subtle English sympathies in the embroidery, which was probably sewn by Anglo-Saxon needleworkers; the balance between Norman propaganda and English perspective is debated.'),
  ('Comets as messages','Across cultures comets were taken as signs of the death of kings or coming wars; the same comet\'s periodic return was only predicted by Edmond Halley in 1705 and confirmed in 1758–59.')],
 ['wiki:Bayeux_Tapestry','wiki:Halley\'s_Comet','wiki:Eilmer_of_Malmesbury'],img='File:Bayeux Tapestry scene32 Halley comet.jpg',dateNote='comet April 1066; tapestry c. 1070s'),
X('ibn-battuta','Ibn Battuta\'s travels (1325–1354)',H,'confirmed',2,1325,['textual'],
 "Ibn Battuta (1304–c. 1368/69), a Muslim legal scholar from Tangier, left home in 1325 to make the pilgrimage to Mecca and kept travelling for nearly 30 years. His journeys took him across North Africa, the Middle East, East Africa (Mogadishu, Kilwa), Anatolia, the Golden Horde, Central Asia, India (where he served as a judge for the Sultan of Delhi), the Maldives, Sri Lanka, possibly China, Al-Andalus and across the Sahara to the Mali Empire. On his return to Morocco he dictated his account, known as the Rihla, to the writer Ibn Juzayy, completed in 1355.",
 [('Trustworthy eyewitness','Historians rely on the Rihla as a unique source on the 14th-century Islamic world, including the Maldives, the Swahili coast and the court of Mansa Sulayman of Mali.'),
  ('Borrowed passages and doubtful trips','Scholars have shown that some passages were copied from the earlier traveller Ibn Jubayr, and many doubt that Ibn Battuta made all the journeys he describes, especially to the Volga Bulgars and to China, which may be based on others\' accounts.'),
  ('Distance record','He is often said to have covered around 117,000 km, more than Marco Polo; the figure is an approximate modern reconstruction.')],
 ['wiki:Ibn_Battuta','wiki:Rihla'],img='Ibn_Battuta',end=1354,dateNote='travels 1325–1354; Rihla completed 1355'),
X('hildegard-of-bingen','Hildegard of Bingen\'s visions',H,'confirmed',2,1141,['textual','iconographic'],
 "Hildegard of Bingen (1098–1179) was a German Benedictine abbess, composer, writer on medicine and natural history, and visionary. In 1141 she began recording the visions she said she had experienced since childhood, which she described as a 'living light', in the book Scivias ('Know the Ways'), completed around 1151 and illustrated with striking images. She also invented an alphabet and a partly constructed language (Lingua ignota), corresponded with popes and emperors, and composed some of the earliest liturgical music whose composer is known by name. She was declared a saint and a Doctor of the Church in 2012.",
 [('Divine revelation','Within her lifetime Pope Eugenius III approved her visions, and Catholic tradition considers them genuine religious experiences.'),
  ('Migraine aura','In 1917 historian Charles Singer, and later neurologist Oliver Sacks, suggested that the shimmering lights, fortifications and falling stars in her descriptions and illustrations resemble migraine aura; others caution against retrospective diagnosis and note that such an explanation does not address the content or meaning of her visions.'),
  ('A woman\'s authority','Historians emphasise that presenting her knowledge as visionary gave a 12th-century woman the authority to teach and write on theology at a time when women were generally barred from doing so.')],
 ['wiki:Hildegard_of_Bingen','wiki:Scivias','wiki:Lingua_ignota'],img='Scivias',end=1179,dateNote='visions recorded from 1141 (Scivias c. 1141–1151)'),
X('melusine','Melusine: the serpent-woman ancestor of noble houses',M,'myth-legend',3,1393,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "Melusine is a female spirit of fresh waters in European folklore, usually depicted as a woman who becomes a serpent or fish from the waist down. The best-known version is the Roman de Mélusine written by Jean d'Arras in 1393 for the Duke of Berry: Melusine marries Raymondin on condition that he never sees her on a Saturday; when he spies on her bathing and discovers her serpent tail, she flies away in the form of a dragon. She was claimed as an ancestor by the House of Lusignan, rulers of Cyprus and Jerusalem, and by other noble families including the counts of Luxembourg.",
 [('Political genealogy','Historians see the romance partly as dynastic propaganda: a supernatural ancestress gave prestige to the Lusignans and helped legitimise claims, including the Duke of Berry\'s to the castle of Lusignan.'),
  ('A widespread pattern','Folklorists classify Melusine among "fairy bride" or supernatural-wife tales with a broken taboo, a pattern also found in Japanese, Celtic and other traditions (for example selkie stories).'),
  ('Older roots','Some scholars connect her with earlier water deities or serpent-goddess traditions; these links are possible but hard to demonstrate.')],
 ['wiki:Melusine','wiki:Jean_d\'Arras'],img='Melusine',dateNote='romance of 1393; older folklore'),
X('mandeville-travels','The Travels of Sir John Mandeville: a bestseller from an armchair',H,'confirmed',3,1357,['textual'],
 "The Book of Sir John Mandeville appeared in French around 1357–1371, claiming to be the account of an English knight who travelled to the Holy Land, Egypt, India, China and beyond between 1322 and 1356. It describes real places alongside wonders such as dog-headed men, headless people with faces in their chests, the land of Prester John and the vegetable lamb of Tartary. It was translated into many languages and was among the most popular books of the late Middle Ages; it argued that the Earth was round and could be circumnavigated.",
 [('Compiled from other books','Scholars have shown that most of the text was compiled from earlier sources, including the travels of Odoric of Pordenone and William of Boldensel and the encyclopedia of Vincent of Beauvais; the author probably travelled little, if at all.'),
  ('Who was "Mandeville"?','The author\'s identity is unknown. Proposals include the Liège physician Jean de Bourgogne and the chronicler Jean d\'Outremeuse; many scholars think "Sir John Mandeville" is a pen name.'),
  ('Influence on explorers','Columbus and other explorers are reported to have known the book, and its round-Earth arguments and accounts of wealthy eastern lands shaped late medieval expectations of the wider world.')],
 ['wiki:The_Travels_of_Sir_John_Mandeville','wiki:Odoric_of_Pordenone'],img='The_Travels_of_Sir_John_Mandeville',dateNote='c. 1357–1371'),
]
# --- part 3: Africa, Asia & the Americas
E+=[
X('hanno-periplus','Hanno the Navigator: a Carthaginian voyage to West Africa and the "gorillas"',H,'disputed',2,-500,['textual'],
 "The Periplus of Hanno is a short Greek text that claims to translate a Punic inscription set up in the temple of Baal Hammon at Carthage. It describes how Hanno sailed through the Strait of Gibraltar with 60 ships, reportedly carrying 30,000 settlers, founding colonies along the Atlantic coast of Morocco and then exploring further south. The account mentions crocodiles and hippopotamuses in a river, a land where fires burned at night, a tall burning mountain called the 'Chariot of the Gods' (Theon Ochema), and hairy 'wild people' whom interpreters called Gorillai; three females were captured, killed and their skins taken back to Carthage. The voyage is usually dated to around 500 BCE.",
 [('As far as Cameroon or Gabon','Many scholars identify the burning mountain with Mount Cameroon, an active volcano, which would mean Hanno reached the Gulf of Guinea; the "gorillas" may have been chimpanzees, gorillas or people.'),
  ('A much shorter voyage','Others argue the realistic part of the voyage ended in southern Morocco, Senegal or Sierra Leone, and that winds and currents along the Saharan coast made a longer return trip very difficult for ancient ships; the text may be shortened, exaggerated or partly literary.'),
  ('The name "gorilla"','In 1847 the American missionary Thomas Savage and naturalist Jeffries Wyman borrowed Hanno\'s word to name the newly described great ape, Gorilla, giving the ancient text a modern afterlife.')],
 ['wiki:Hanno_the_Navigator','wiki:Mount_Cameroon'],img='File:The Periplus of Hanno - Map to illustrate the Periplus of Hanno.png',dateNote='c. 500 BCE (5th c. BCE; date uncertain)'),
X('mutapa-ophir','The Mutapa kingdom and the European search for King Solomon\'s Ophir',H,'confirmed',2,1430,['textual','archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "The Mutapa (Mwenemutapa or Monomotapa) state was a Shona kingdom founded in the 15th century, by tradition by Nyatsimba Mutota, in the region between the Zambezi and Limpopo rivers in present-day Zimbabwe and Mozambique. It grew rich from gold and ivory trade with Swahili merchants on the Indian Ocean coast, and from the 16th century the Portuguese tried to control its gold, eventually making the kingdom a vassal in 1629. It declined in the 17th and 18th centuries. Portuguese writers from the 16th century onward speculated that its gold-producing lands, and the stone ruins of the interior, were the biblical Ophir, from where King Solomon's ships brought gold.",
 [('Ophir in Africa?','The location of the biblical Ophir is unknown; proposals include southern Arabia, the Horn of Africa, India and southern Africa. There is no archaeological evidence connecting Solomon or the Queen of Sheba to Mutapa or Great Zimbabwe.'),
  ('Colonial myth-making','The German geologist Karl Mauch (1871) and later Rhodesian colonial writers attributed Great Zimbabwe to Phoenicians or Sheba; archaeologists from David Randall-MacIver (1905) and Gertrude Caton-Thompson (1929) onward showed that the stone towns were built by ancestors of the Shona, and the colonial denial is now studied as politically motivated.'),
  ('Literature','The Ophir legend inspired H. Rider Haggard\'s novel King Solomon\'s Mines (1885), which helped spread the idea of lost white or foreign builders in Africa.')],
 ['wiki:Mutapa_Empire','wiki:Ophir'],img='Mutapa_Empire',end=1760,dateNote='c. 1430–1760'),
X('battle-of-adwa','Battle of Adwa (1896): an African army defeats a European invasion',H,'confirmed',2,1896,['textual'],
 "On 1 March 1896 the Ethiopian army of Emperor Menelik II, with Empress Taytu Betul and regional commanders, decisively defeated an invading Italian army under General Oreste Baratieri near Adwa in northern Ethiopia. The war had grown out of a dispute over the 1889 Treaty of Wuchale, whose Italian text made Ethiopia a protectorate while the Amharic text did not. Italy lost several thousand men killed, and the Treaty of Addis Ababa (October 1896) recognised Ethiopian independence. Ethiopia remained independent while the rest of Africa was colonised, until the Italian invasion of 1935–36.",
 [('Symbol of African independence','Adwa became a major symbol for Pan-African and anti-colonial movements; the colours and history of Ethiopia influenced movements in the Caribbean and the Americas, including Rastafari.'),
  ('Divine help','Ethiopian Orthodox tradition holds that the tabot (consecrated altar tablet) of St George was carried to the battle and that the saint aided the Ethiopian army; the victory is commemorated on St George\'s feast and in Addis Ababa\'s St George\'s Cathedral.'),
  ('Why Ethiopia won','Historians point to Menelik\'s large, well-armed army (with modern rifles bought partly from Europe), Ethiopian unity, poor Italian intelligence and maps, and Baratieri\'s decision to attack; Italian nationalist accounts long blamed individual generals.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Adwa','wiki:Treaty_of_Wuchale','wiki:Menelik_II'],img='Battle_of_Adwa',dateNote='1 March 1896'),
X('heike-crabs','Heike crabs: samurai faces on crab shells',M,'myth-legend',3,1185,['oral tradition','biological'],
 "Heikea japonica is a small crab of Japanese waters whose shell bears ridges and grooves resembling an angry human face. Japanese legend says the crabs carry the spirits of the Taira (Heike) samurai who drowned at the naval Battle of Dan-no-ura in 1185, when the Taira clan was destroyed by the Minamoto and the child emperor Antoku died in the sea.",
 [('Artificial selection by fishermen','In 1952 biologist Julian Huxley proposed that fishermen had for centuries thrown back crabs with the most face-like shells out of respect, unintentionally selecting for human-like faces. Carl Sagan popularised the idea in Cosmos (1980) as an example of selection.'),
  ('Ordinary anatomy and pareidolia','Critics, including crab biologist Joel W. Martin (1993), note that these crabs are generally not eaten (so there is little reason for fishermen to select them), that the ridges mark muscle attachments, and that related species and fossils show similar patterns; the face is seen as an example of human pattern-seeking (pareidolia).'),
  ('Living memory','Whatever the biology, the legend keeps the memory of the Genpei War alive, alongside the ghost stories of Hōichi the Earless set at the same strait.')],
 ['wiki:Heikegani','wiki:Battle_of_Dan-no-ura'],img='Heikegani',dateNote='legend linked to the battle of 1185'),
X('ainu','The Ainu: Japan\'s Indigenous people and the debate over their origins',H,'confirmed',2,1300,['archaeological','genetic','oral tradition','textual'],
 "The Ainu are the Indigenous people of Hokkaido, and historically also of Sakhalin, the Kuril Islands and northern Honshu. Ainu culture, with its own language (an isolate), bear ceremony (iomante), oral epics (yukar) and trade networks reaching Manchuria, is usually traced from around the 13th century, developing out of the Satsumon and Okhotsk cultures. The Japanese Matsumae domain increasingly controlled trade with them; Shakushain's revolt (1669) was suppressed. After 1869 Hokkaido was colonised, and the Hokkaido Former Aborigines Protection Act of 1899 promoted assimilation. Japan's parliament recognised the Ainu as an Indigenous people in 2008, and a 2019 law gave them legal recognition.",
 [('Jōmon descendants','Genetic and skeletal studies indicate that the Ainu descend largely from Jōmon-period populations of the Japanese archipelago, with later contributions from the Okhotsk culture of the Sea of Okhotsk region.'),
  ('Discarded "Caucasoid" theories','Nineteenth- and early 20th-century anthropologists, impressed by facial features and body hair, claimed the Ainu were a lost European or "Caucasian" people; modern genetics has rejected this.'),
  ('The original Japanese?','Some writers argue that Ainu or Jōmon peoples were the original inhabitants of all Japan and that their role has been downplayed in national history; placenames of possible Ainu origin in northern Honshu support a former presence there, but the wider picture remains debated.')],
 ['wiki:Ainu_people','wiki:Shakushain\'s_revolt','wiki:Ainu_language'],img='Ainu_people',dateNote='Ainu culture from c. 13th c.; recognised as Indigenous 2008/2019'),
X('peach-blossom-spring','The Peach Blossom Spring: a hidden valley outside time',M,'myth-legend',2,421,['textual'],
 "The Peach Blossom Spring is a short prose fable written by the Chinese poet Tao Yuanming (Tao Qian) in 421 CE. A fisherman from Wuling follows a stream lined with blossoming peach trees to a cave, and passes through into a peaceful farming valley whose people say their ancestors fled the chaos of the Qin dynasty centuries earlier; they know nothing of the Han or later dynasties. They feed him and ask him not to tell anyone. He marks his route, but when he returns with officials the place can never be found again. The phrase 'peach blossom spring' (taohuayuan) became the standard Chinese expression for a hidden utopia.",
 [('Pure fiction and philosophy','Most literary scholars read it as a Daoist-influenced allegory of escape from war and politics, written by a poet who had himself withdrawn from office.'),
  ('Real refugee communities','The historian Chen Yinke (1936) argued that the story reflects real fortified refugee settlements (wubao) that existed in northern China during the wars of the 4th century; others think the parallels are general rather than specific.'),
  ('Shangri-La and other hidden valleys','The tale belongs to a worldwide pattern of hidden valleys outside time, from Tibetan beyul and Shambhala to James Hilton\'s Shangri-La (1933); whether such stories reflect isolated real communities, religious ideals or simple storytelling varies by case.')],
 ['wiki:The_Peach_Blossom_Spring','wiki:Tao_Yuanming'],img='The_Peach_Blossom_Spring',dateNote='written 421 CE'),
X('hampi-vijayanagara','Hampi, the city of victory, and Hanuman\'s birthplace',H,'confirmed',2,1336,['archaeological','textual','oral tradition'],
 "Hampi in Karnataka, India, was the core of Vijayanagara, capital of the Vijayanagara Empire, founded around 1336 by the brothers Harihara and Bukka of the Sangama dynasty. By the 15th–16th centuries it was among the largest cities in the world, described with wonder by visitors such as the Persian envoy Abdur Razzaq and the Portuguese Domingo Paes. After the empire's defeat by an alliance of Deccan sultanates at the Battle of Talikota in 1565, the city was sacked and abandoned. Its temples, bazaars and royal enclosures among granite boulders are a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1986).",
 [('Founded with a sage\'s guidance','Tradition credits the sage Vidyaranya with inspiring the founding of the city; historians debate the extent of his role, which is mainly documented in later sources.'),
  ('Kishkindha of the Ramayana','Local tradition identifies the area with Kishkindha, the monkey kingdom of the Ramayana, and Anjanadri hill near Hampi as the birthplace of Hanuman; other places in India (for example in Andhra Pradesh and Jharkhand) also claim the birthplace. This is a matter of faith and tradition, not archaeology.'),
  ('Why did it fall so completely?','Some historians emphasise the battle and the following sack, others longer-term political divisions, the betrayal of Muslim commanders in Vijayanagara service, or shifts in trade.')],
 ['wiki:Hampi','wiki:Vijayanagara','wiki:Battle_of_Talikota','url:'+U+'241/|Group of Monuments at Hampi (UNESCO)|heritage'],img='Hampi',end=1565,dateNote='founded c. 1336; sacked 1565'),
X('monte-alban','Monte Albán and the Danzantes: dancers, captives or something else?',H,'confirmed',2,-500,['archaeological'],
 "Monte Albán, on a flattened mountain ridge above the Valley of Oaxaca, Mexico, was founded around 500 BCE and became the capital of the Zapotec state for over a thousand years, declining by about 750–850 CE. Among its earliest monuments are more than 300 carved stone slabs called the Danzantes ('dancers'), showing naked men in contorted poses, some with closed eyes and mutilated bodies, along with some of the earliest writing in Mesoamerica. Building J, shaped like an arrowhead, is set at an unusual angle to the rest of the plaza. In 1932 Alfonso Caso found Tomb 7, reused by Mixtec people and filled with gold, jade and carved bone. Monte Albán and Oaxaca's historic centre are a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1987).",
 [('Captives, not dancers','Most archaeologists now interpret the Danzantes as slain or sacrificed war captives, displayed to show Monte Albán\'s military power; the name reflects 19th-century impressions.'),
  ('Other readings','Earlier interpretations saw them as dancers, swimmers, people with medical conditions, or shamans in trance; some fringe writers have also claimed they show foreign peoples, a view not supported by archaeologists.'),
  ('Building J and the sky','Building J\'s orientation has been linked by archaeoastronomers to the rising of the star Capella and to the zenith passage of the sun; others see the shape as a conquest monument listing subject towns. The two ideas are not exclusive.')],
 ['wiki:Monte_Albán','wiki:Zapotec_civilization','url:'+U+'415/|Historic Centre of Oaxaca and Archaeological Site of Monte Albán (UNESCO)|heritage'],img='Monte_Albán',end=850,dateNote='c. 500 BCE – c. 750/850 CE'),
X('pueblo-revolt','The Pueblo Revolt of 1680',H,'confirmed',2,1680,['textual','archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "On 10 August 1680 the Pueblo peoples of New Mexico rose in a coordinated revolt against Spanish colonial rule, which had suppressed their religion and imposed forced labour and tribute. The leader most associated with it, Po'pay of Ohkay Owingeh, had been flogged in 1675 for 'sorcery'. Runners carried knotted cords to the pueblos to count down the days to the uprising. About 400 Spanish colonists and 21 Franciscan friars were killed, Santa Fe was besieged, and the surviving Spanish withdrew to El Paso. The Pueblos remained independent for 12 years until Diego de Vargas reconquered the region from 1692.",
 [('Spirit guidance','Spanish testimony records that Po\'pay claimed to receive instructions in a kiva at Taos from spirit beings; some Pueblo traditions remember the revolt as a restoration of religious and cosmic order.'),
  ('First American revolution?','Some historians and Pueblo people call it the first successful revolution in what is now the United States; others note it was a coalition of many independent communities with varied aims, which later divided.'),
  ('Lasting effects','Although the Spanish returned, many historians argue that the revolt led to greater tolerance of Pueblo religion and land rights, helping Pueblo cultures survive. A statue of Po\'pay was placed in the US Capitol in 2005.')],
 ['wiki:Pueblo_Revolt','wiki:Po\'pay','wiki:Diego_de_Vargas'],img='Po\'pay',dateNote='10 August 1680; reconquest from 1692'),
X('fountain-of-youth','Ponce de León and the Fountain of Youth: a legend added later',M,'myth-legend',2,1513,['textual'],
 "The Spanish conquistador Juan Ponce de León led the first recorded European expedition to Florida in 1513. He is widely said to have been searching for a Fountain of Youth. However, his royal contract and contemporary records say nothing about such a fountain; the link first appears in the writings of the royal chronicler Gonzalo Fernández de Oviedo (1535), who appears to have mocked him with it, and was repeated by later writers such as Hernando de Escalante Fontaneda and Antonio de Herrera. Stories of waters that restore youth are far older, found in Herodotus (the long-lived Ethiopians' spring), the Alexander Romance and medieval European legends.",
 [('Myth about history','Most historians conclude that Ponce de León was seeking land, gold and a governorship, and that the fountain story was attached to him after his death, partly to ridicule him.'),
  ('Indigenous rumours','Some sources say Taíno or other Indigenous people told Spaniards of a restorative spring or river (sometimes called Bimini); scholars debate whether such reports reflect real Indigenous traditions or European expectations.'),
  ('Tourist site','In St. Augustine, Florida, a "Fountain of Youth Archaeological Park", promoted since the early 20th century, has real colonial and Timucua archaeology but no evidence of a magical spring or of Ponce de León\'s landing there.')],
 ['wiki:Fountain_of_Youth','wiki:Juan_Ponce_de_León'],img='Fountain_of_Youth',dateNote='expedition 1513; fountain story first attested 1535'),
]
# --- part 4: 18th–20th c. mysteries, fringe theories, hoaxes and the science of life's origins
E+=[
X('oak-island','The Oak Island Money Pit',H,'disputed',2,1795,['textual','archaeological','geological'],
 "According to the most widely told story, in 1795 a young man named Daniel McGinnis found a circular depression on Oak Island, Nova Scotia, and with friends dug down through layers of logs. Later treasure companies (from about 1803 onward) reported platforms of logs at regular intervals, a stone with strange symbols at around 90 feet (now lost, with no surviving photograph), and flooding by seawater, which some interpreted as deliberately engineered booby-trap tunnels. Excavations over two centuries have produced few datable finds; six people have died in accidents, including four in 1965 overcome by gas in a shaft. Since 2014 the TV series The Curse of Oak Island has followed a new search.",
 [('Hidden treasure','Proposed treasures include pirate gold (Captain Kidd, Blackbeard), Spanish or British military payrolls, French crown jewels, Knights Templar relics or the Holy Grail, and manuscripts proving Francis Bacon wrote Shakespeare. None has been found, and these remain speculative.'),
  ('Natural sinkhole','Geologists note that the area contains soluble anhydrite and gypsum layers that form natural sinkholes and caverns, and that seawater infiltration through porous rock can explain the flooding; many early "platform" reports could be fallen trees or debris.'),
  ('Embellished early accounts','Historians have found that the earliest detailed written accounts date from the 1860s, decades after the claimed discovery, and that details such as the inscribed stone grew in retelling.')],
 ['wiki:Oak_Island_mystery','wiki:Oak_Island'],img='Oak_Island_mystery',dateNote='first reported dig 1795 (accounts written from 1860s)'),
X('beale-ciphers','The Beale ciphers: buried treasure or 1885 hoax?',H,'disputed',2,1885,['textual','speculative'],
 "The Beale Papers, a pamphlet published in Lynchburg, Virginia, in 1885 by James B. Ward, tells how a man named Thomas J. Beale buried a large treasure of gold, silver and jewels in Bedford County, Virginia, around 1819–1821, and left three encrypted messages with an innkeeper, Robert Morriss. The pamphlet says an unnamed friend decoded the second cipher using the United States Declaration of Independence as a key, revealing a description of the treasure; the first (location) and third (names of owners) have never been solved.",
 [('Real treasure, unsolved code','Treasure hunters and amateur cryptographers have searched and dug in Bedford County for over a century, believing the first and third ciphers conceal a genuine location.'),
  ('Hoax','Statistical analyses of the unsolved ciphers suggest patterns unlikely in real encrypted text, and writer Joe Nickell (1982) pointed to anachronistic words such as "stampeding" in Beale\'s supposed letters; no record of Thomas J. Beale matching the story has been firmly established. Many researchers conclude the pamphlet was a fiction, possibly a money-making scheme or a Masonic-style allegory.'),
  ('Still open','Supporters argue that the absence of a solution does not prove a hoax, and the ciphers remain a popular challenge in cryptography courses.')],
 ['wiki:Beale_ciphers'],img='Beale_ciphers',dateNote='pamphlet 1885; story set 1819–1822'),
X('symmes-hollow-earth','Symmes\'s hole: the Hollow Earth with openings at the poles',H,'debunked',2,1818,['textual','speculative'],
 "In 1818 John Cleves Symmes Jr., a former US Army officer, sent a circular to scientists and governments declaring 'I declare the earth is hollow, and habitable within', containing concentric spheres open at the poles. He lectured widely and petitioned Congress to fund an expedition to the polar opening. Hollow-Earth ideas had a scientific pedigree: in 1692 the astronomer Edmond Halley proposed concentric shells inside the Earth to explain shifts in the magnetic field. Symmes's follower Jeremiah Reynolds helped build support for polar exploration that fed into the United States Exploring Expedition (1838–42).",
 [('Disproved by geophysics','Seismology from the early 20th century (Richard Oldham\'s detection of the core in 1906, Inge Lehmann\'s inner core in 1936) and gravity measurements show a dense solid inner core and liquid outer core, incompatible with a hollow Earth; polar regions have been mapped and crossed without openings.'),
  ('Modern versions','Hollow-Earth beliefs survive in some esoteric and conspiracy traditions, often combined with Agartha, Shambhala, Nazi-escape stories or Admiral Byrd\'s polar flights; these are not supported by evidence.'),
  ('Literary legacy','The idea inspired fiction such as Symzonia (1820), Edgar Allan Poe\'s work and Jules Verne\'s Journey to the Centre of the Earth (1864).')],
 ['wiki:John_Cleves_Symmes_Jr.','wiki:Hollow_Earth','wiki:United_States_Exploring_Expedition'],img='Hollow_Earth',dateNote='circular of 1818',
 changed='1692: Halley proposes concentric shells. 1818: Symmes adds polar openings. 1906–1936: seismology reveals solid inner and liquid outer core, ruling out a hollow Earth.'),
X('shaver-mystery','The Shaver Mystery: "I Remember Lemuria!" and the deros underground',H,'debunked',3,1945,['textual','speculative'],
 "In March 1945 the pulp magazine Amazing Stories, edited by Ray Palmer, published 'I Remember Lemuria!' by Richard Sharpe Shaver, a Pennsylvania welder. Presented as fiction but promoted as true, it claimed that cave systems beneath the Earth were inhabited by 'deros', degenerate descendants of an ancient super-race, who used advanced machines to torment surface people with voices, accidents and evil thoughts. The magazine received thousands of letters from readers claiming similar experiences, and sales rose sharply. Shaver had earlier spent time in a psychiatric hospital, a fact not widely known to readers at the time.",
 [('Psychological explanation','Many writers interpret Shaver\'s "voices" as symptoms of mental illness, and the reader response as a mix of play, belief and shared anxieties of the war years.'),
  ('A bridge to flying saucers','Historians of the UFO phenomenon note that Palmer, after the Kenneth Arnold sighting of 1947, shifted from the Shaver Mystery to flying saucers, co-founding Fate magazine in 1948, and that Shaver material helped prepare an audience for UFO narratives.'),
  ('Believers','A small group of supporters continued to believe in Shaver\'s caves; Shaver himself later claimed that patterns in cut rocks were "rock books" left by the ancient race. These claims are not supported by evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Richard_Sharpe_Shaver'],img='Richard_Sharpe_Shaver',dateNote='March 1945'),
X('ley-lines','Ley lines: from old straight tracks to earth energies',H,'debunked',2,1921,['textual','speculative'],
 "In 1921 the English businessman and antiquarian Alfred Watkins noticed that ancient sites in Herefordshire, such as standing stones, hillforts, churches and crossroads, seemed to fall on straight lines. In The Old Straight Track (1925) he proposed that these 'leys' marked prehistoric trackways laid out by surveyors. Archaeologists did not accept the idea. In the 1960s ley lines were revived in a new form, especially through John Michell's The View Over Atlantis (1969), as lines of earth energy, sometimes linked to UFO flight paths, dowsing and ancient wisdom; a 'ley hunter' movement grew up around them.",
 [('Chance alignments','Statisticians and archaeologists have shown that in a landscape dense with ancient and medieval sites, many straight alignments of three or more points are expected by chance; the sites on a given ley are also often of widely different dates. Archaeologists generally classify ley lines as pseudoarchaeology.'),
  ('Real ancient alignments','Some genuine prehistoric alignments do exist, for example monuments oriented to solstice sunrise or sunset (as Norman Lockyer argued) or Roman roads, but these are specific and testable rather than a network.'),
  ('Earth energies','Many practitioners of dowsing, earth mysteries and New Age spirituality regard ley lines as real channels of subtle energy; no instrument has detected such energy, so the claim remains a matter of belief.')],
 ['wiki:Ley_line','wiki:Alfred_Watkins','wiki:John_Michell_(writer)'],img='Ley_line',dateNote='Watkins\'s insight 1921; book 1925; revival 1960s'),
X('velikovsky-worlds-in-collision','Velikovsky\'s Worlds in Collision (1950)',H,'debunked',2,1950,['textual','speculative'],
 "In 1950 the psychiatrist Immanuel Velikovsky published Worlds in Collision, arguing that around 1500 BCE a comet ejected from Jupiter passed close to Earth, causing the plagues of Egypt, the parting of the sea and manna, before settling as the planet Venus; later close passes of Mars caused catastrophes in the 8th–7th centuries BCE. He drew mainly on myths and ancient texts from many cultures. The book became a bestseller, but astronomers, led by Harlow Shapley, reacted fiercely; after threats of a textbook boycott, the publisher Macmillan transferred it to Doubleday within two months. Velikovsky also proposed a radically revised chronology of ancient Egypt in Ages in Chaos (1952).",
 [('Physically impossible','Astronomers argue that the orbital mechanics, the composition of Venus and Earth\'s geological and ice-core records rule out the proposed events; Carl Sagan presented a detailed critique at the 1974 AAAS symposium.'),
  ('Successful predictions?','Supporters point to his statements that Venus would be hot and that Jupiter would emit radio noise, which were later confirmed; critics reply that these were not uniquely derived from his theory, and that many of his other predictions failed.'),
  ('Suppression or quality control','The campaign against the book is often cited as an example of scientific establishment suppression; others see it as a heavy-handed but legitimate rejection of unsupported ideas. The episode influenced later debates on catastrophism, cosmic impacts and the sociology of science.')],
 ['wiki:Immanuel_Velikovsky','wiki:Worlds_in_Collision'],img='Immanuel_Velikovsky',dateNote='published April 1950'),
X('versailles-time-slip-1901','The Versailles "time slip" (1901)',H,'disputed',2,1901,['textual'],
 "On 10 August 1901 two English academics, Charlotte Anne Moberly, principal of St Hugh's Hall, Oxford, and Eleanor Jourdain, visited the gardens of the Petit Trianon at Versailles. They later reported feeling oppressed and seeing people in 18th-century dress, including a woman sketching whom Moberly came to believe was Marie Antoinette. After research they concluded they had somehow entered the queen's memories or the year 1789. In 1911 they published their account, An Adventure, under the pseudonyms Elizabeth Morison and Frances Lamont; it became one of the most famous ghost or time-slip stories.",
 [('Costume party','In 1965 Philippe Jullian suggested that the women had stumbled on a costume rehearsal or party organised by the poet Robert de Montesquiou, who lived nearby and reportedly staged such events in period dress.'),
  ('Memory and suggestion','Psychical researchers and sceptics noted that the account was written down months later, revised over years and shaped by shared discussion and historical reading, making it unreliable as evidence.'),
  ('Genuine paranormal experience','Believers in time slips regard it as a classic case of retrocognition, pointing to the authors\' respectability and apparent sincerity; the claim cannot be tested.')],
 ['wiki:Moberly–Jourdain_incident'],img='Moberly–Jourdain_incident',dateNote='10 August 1901; book 1911'),
X('princess-caraboo','Princess Caraboo of Javasu (1817)',H,'debunked',3,1817,['textual'],
 "In April 1817 a young woman in exotic dress appeared in Almondsbury, near Bristol, England, speaking an unknown language. With the help of a Portuguese sailor who claimed to understand her, she was presented as Princess Caraboo from the island of Javasu in the Indian Ocean, kidnapped by pirates before escaping by jumping overboard. She was taken in by a local magistrate's family, fenced, swam, prayed to a god she called 'Allah-Talla' and became a sensation. A boarding-house keeper, Mrs Neale, recognised her from a newspaper portrait as Mary Baker (née Willcocks), a cobbler's daughter from Devon. She left for Philadelphia in 1817 and in later life sold leeches to Bristol Infirmary.",
 [('Skilled impostor','Most accounts present her as a talented performer who exploited Regency fascination with the exotic East; how she sustained an invented language and customs for weeks remains impressive.'),
  ('Experts fooled','The case is often cited as an example of how "experts" can project their expectations onto a mystery: visitors identified her language and writing with various Asian scripts.'),
  ('Poverty and survival','Social historians emphasise that she had endured poverty, illness and servitude, and that the imposture offered shelter and attention.')],
 ['wiki:Princess_Caraboo'],img='Princess_Caraboo',dateNote='April–June 1817'),
X('origin-of-species-1859','Darwin\'s On the Origin of Species (1859)',H,'confirmed',1,1859,['textual','biological','fossil'],
 "Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species on 24 November 1859, arguing that species change over time and descend from common ancestors through natural selection: individuals with heritable variations better suited to their environment leave more offspring. The idea had been presented jointly with Alfred Russel Wallace, who reached it independently, at the Linnean Society in 1858. The book provoked intense debate, including the 1860 Oxford exchange between Thomas Huxley and Bishop Samuel Wilberforce. Combined with genetics in the 'modern synthesis' of the 1930s–40s and supported by fossils, comparative anatomy and DNA, evolution by natural selection became the foundation of modern biology.",
 [('Mainstream science','Biologists regard common descent as overwhelmingly confirmed; debates continue on the relative roles of selection, genetic drift, development and other mechanisms.'),
  ('Religious views','Many religious traditions and believers accept evolution as compatible with faith (theistic evolution); others hold to special creation or young-Earth creationism, and the intelligent-design movement argues some features require a designer. Courts in the USA (e.g. Kitzmiller v. Dover, 2005) ruled intelligent design to be religious rather than science.'),
  ('Earlier evolutionary ideas','Ideas of transformation of species existed before Darwin, from ancient Greek and medieval Islamic thinkers to Lamarck and Darwin\'s grandfather Erasmus; Darwin\'s contribution was a mechanism backed by extensive evidence.')],
 ['wiki:On_the_Origin_of_Species','wiki:Alfred_Russel_Wallace','wiki:1860_Oxford_evolution_debate'],img='On_the_Origin_of_Species',dateNote='24 November 1859'),
X('miller-urey-1953','The Miller–Urey experiment (1952–53): making life\'s building blocks in a flask',H,'confirmed',2,1953,['experimental'],
 "In 1952 Stanley Miller, a graduate student working with Harold Urey at the University of Chicago, passed electric sparks through a mixture of methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water vapour meant to imitate the early Earth's atmosphere. Within days the water turned brown and contained amino acids such as glycine and alanine, the building blocks of proteins. The results, published in Science in May 1953, launched the experimental study of the origin of life (abiogenesis). In 2008 a re-analysis of Miller's archived samples with modern instruments found more than 20 amino acids, especially in a variant simulating a volcanic eruption.",
 [('What was the early atmosphere?','Many geochemists later concluded that the early atmosphere was less reducing (more carbon dioxide and nitrogen) than Miller assumed, which yields fewer amino acids; volcanic plumes or local environments may still have been suitable.'),
  ('Other origin-of-life scenarios','Researchers also study deep-sea hydrothermal vents, hot springs on land, an "RNA world" in which self-copying RNA came first, mineral surfaces and delivery of organics by meteorites (amino acids have been found in meteorites such as Murchison).'),
  ('Panspermia and creation','Some scientists propose that life or its precursors arrived from space (panspermia); religious traditions attribute life to divine creation. How life first arose remains unresolved in science.')],
 ['doi:10.1126/science.117.3046.528','doi:10.1126/science.1161527','wiki:Miller–Urey_experiment','wiki:Abiogenesis'],img='Miller–Urey_experiment',dateNote='experiment 1952; published 15 May 1953'),
]
